{
  "title": "Operators, precedence and formatted I/O",
  "kicker": "ENCT 101 · Computer Programming · Chapters 3–4",
  "subtitle": "Expressions are evaluated in a fixed order. Know it, and you can predict any line of arithmetic.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "arith",
      "locator": "3.1",
      "title": "Arithmetic operators",
      "eyebrow": "+ − * / %",
      "body": "<p><code>+ - * /</code> and the remainder <code>%</code>. With two integers, <code>/</code> is <b>integer division</b> (truncates): 7/2 is 3, not 3.5; <code>%</code> gives the leftover: 7 % 3 is 1. To get 3.5 make one operand a double (7/2.0).</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "div",
          "prompt": "9 / 4 in C (both int) is…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "2.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "2.25."
            },
            {
              "text": "3."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "The fraction is discarded."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Needs a double."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Rounding is not done."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "cpp1",
          "caption": "Figure 3.1.1 — Arithmetic operators",
          "note": "Integer division truncates toward zero.",
          "script": "var lab = create(\"dsa\", {\"name\":\"prog\",\"source\":\"#include <iostream>\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main() {\\n  cout << 7 / 2 << \\\" \\\" << 7 % 3 << \\\" \\\" << -7 / 2 << endl;\\n  return 0;\\n}\\n\",\"width\":760,\"height\":520});",
          "expect": {
            "prog": {
              "output": "3 1 -3"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "prec",
      "locator": "3.2",
      "title": "Precedence and associativity",
      "eyebrow": "Who goes first",
      "body": "<p>From high to low: parentheses, unary (<code>! - ++ --</code>), <code>* / %</code>, <code>+ -</code>, relational (<code>&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;=</code>), equality (<code>== !=</code>), <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, assignment. Equal precedence goes left to right (assignment right to left). When unsure, add parentheses.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Plan",
          "why": "Evaluate 2 + 3 * 4 − 6 / 2."
        },
        {
          "toc": "Reduce",
          "why": "Multiplication and division first: 12 and 3.",
          "math": "2+12-3"
        },
        {
          "toc": "Result",
          "why": "Then left to right.",
          "math": "=11",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "pr",
          "prompt": "5 + 2 * 3 − 4 =",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "17."
            },
            {
              "text": "13."
            },
            {
              "text": "7.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Did + before *."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Left to right without precedence."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "5 + 6 − 4."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "cpp2",
          "caption": "Figure 3.2.1 — Precedence and associativity",
          "note": "* before +; parentheses override.",
          "script": "var lab = create(\"dsa\", {\"name\":\"prog\",\"source\":\"#include <iostream>\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main() {\\n  int a = 5, b = 3;\\n  cout << a + b * 2 << \\\" \\\" << (a + b) * 2 << \\\" \\\" << (a > b && b < 4) << endl;\\n  return 0;\\n}\\n\",\"width\":760,\"height\":520});",
          "expect": {
            "prog": {
              "output": "11 16 1"
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "problems": [
        {
          "id": "p-pr",
          "prompt": "<p>Evaluate 10 − 4 / 2 * 3 + 1.</p>",
          "answer": "4/2 = 2; 2·3 = 6; 10 − 6 + 1 = <b>5</b>."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "incr",
      "locator": "3.3",
      "title": "Increment, compound assignment, conversion",
      "eyebrow": "Shortcuts and casts",
      "body": "<p><code>x += 5</code> means <code>x = x + 5</code>. <code>i++</code> uses i then adds 1; <code>++i</code> adds 1 then uses it. In mixed expressions the smaller type is promoted (int → double). A cast forces it: <code>(double)a / b</code>.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "inc",
          "prompt": "int i = 2; int j = i++ + 3; leaves j =",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "5.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "6."
            },
            {
              "text": "3."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "i++ yields 2, then i becomes 3."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Would need ++i."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Ignores i."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "cpp3",
          "caption": "Figure 3.3.1 — Increment, compound assignment, conversion",
          "note": "Post-increment yields the old value; pre-increment the new one.",
          "script": "var lab = create(\"dsa\", {\"name\":\"prog\",\"source\":\"#include <iostream>\\nusing namespace std;\\nint main() {\\n  int i = 5;\\n  int a = i++;\\n  int b = ++i;\\n  cout << a << \\\" \\\" << b << \\\" \\\" << i << endl;\\n  return 0;\\n}\\n\",\"width\":760,\"height\":520});",
          "expect": {
            "prog": {
              "output": "5 7 7"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "logic",
      "locator": "3.4",
      "title": "Relational, logical and bitwise",
      "eyebrow": "True and false",
      "body": "<p>In C, 0 is false and any non-zero is true; comparisons yield 0 or 1. <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> short-circuit: in <code>a != 0 &amp;&amp; x/a &gt; 1</code> the division is never attempted when a is 0. Bitwise: <code>&amp; | ^ ~ &lt;&lt; &gt;&gt;</code> work on bits: <code>x &lt;&lt; 1</code> doubles an integer.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Align",
          "why": "12 = 1100₂, 10 = 1010₂. AND bit by bit.",
          "math": "1100\\ \\&\\ 1010"
        },
        {
          "toc": "AND",
          "why": "Result 1000₂.",
          "math": "=8",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "sc",
          "prompt": "Why is <code>p != NULL &amp;&amp; *p &gt; 0</code> safe?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "C checks pointers automatically."
            },
            {
              "text": "It is not safe."
            },
            {
              "text": "&amp;&amp; short-circuits, so *p is skipped when p is NULL.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "It does not."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "It is the standard idiom."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Right side is evaluated only if the left is true."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "io",
      "locator": "4.1",
      "title": "printf and scanf",
      "eyebrow": "Talking to the user",
      "body": "<p><code>printf(\"fmt\", args)</code> writes formatted output; conversion specifiers: <code>%d</code> int, <code>%f</code> double/float, <code>%c</code> char, <code>%s</code> string, <code>%x</code> hex; <code>%5.2f</code> means width 5, 2 decimals. <code>scanf(\"%d\", &amp;n)</code> reads input — note the <b>&amp;</b>: scanf needs the variable’s <i>address</i> so it can store into it. <code>getchar/putchar</code> handle single characters.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "amp",
          "prompt": "Why does scanf need <code>&amp;n</code>?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "To print n."
            },
            {
              "text": "It must know where in memory to store the value.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "It is optional."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "That is printf."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "C passes by value, so the address is passed."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Omitting it is a bug."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "dia4",
          "caption": "Figure 4.1.1 — scanf then printf",
          "note": "Input flows into memory; output flows from it.",
          "script": "diagram(\"mode: sequence\\n[Keyboard] as k\\n[scanf] as s\\n[Variable n] as n\\n[printf] as p\\n[Screen] as c\\nk -> s : 42\\ns -> n : store at &n\\nn -> p : read n\\np -> c : \\\"n = 42\\\"\\n@0 k -> s : 42\\n@1 s -> n : store at &n\\n@2 n -> p : read n\\n@3 p -> c : \\\"n = 42\\\"\");"
        }
      ]
    },
